    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cell size is an important determinant of animal cell function because it sets the rate of biosynthesis and surface transport within the cell. Though there exists a vast range of cell sizes, cells of the same tissue type typically grow to a similar size before progressing through the G1/S checkpoint to duplicate DNA and then divide. Phosphorylation of the retinoblastoma (Rb) tumor suppressor protein has long been known to regulate cell cycle entry at the G1/S checkpoint, and more recently, dilution of Rb by cell growth was proposed as a mechanism through which cell growth triggers the G1/S transition. In addition, it was recently shown that the p38 mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) could also contribute to cell size control with regulation of G1 length. p38 had previously been known to respond to stress stimuli such as cytokines, UV radiation, heat and osmotic shock and involved in cell differentiation, apoptosis, and autophagy. In these studies, p38 inhibited progression through G1 in small cells and not large cells, but not much is known about how it does so. Understanding the mechanism of size control by p38 operates and its relative importance will provide further insight into whether there exists a relationship with the established Rb phosphorylation and Rb dilution G1/S control pathways. Here, by using models of human mammary epithelial cells (HMEC) and retinal pigment epithelium cells (RPE), we observe that size control exists in only select cell lines cultured in vitro. For cell lines exhibiting size control, there is a dose dependent relation to p38 inhibitor drug treatments, confirming a p38 MAPK size control pathway. Furthermore, through analyses of p38 inhibitor and rapamycin drug treatments of wild-type and Rb-knocked out RPE cells, we demonstrate that the role of the p38 MAPK pathway in cell size control does not require Rb. These findings elucidate a crucial mechanism of how cell growth drives cell division that may prove important in further understanding of both development and disease.",
